<td id="S2.T1.7.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}}^{\top}+\sum_{i=k+1}^{n-k}\sigma_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐀𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}}^{\top}+\sum_{i=k+1}^{n-k}\sigma_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In II Related Work for Benchmarking ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>)</td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}(\mathbf{\Sigma}+\underline{\mathbf{M}})\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}(\mathbf{\Sigma}+\underline{\mathbf{M}})\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In II Related Work for Benchmarking ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>)</td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{(}{\Sigma+\underline{\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}}})\underline{\mathbf{G}}\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝚫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{(}{\Sigma+\underline{\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}}})\underline{\mathbf{G}}\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In III SSVD Method ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>)</td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.7.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.7.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times(m+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times(m+n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+m+1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times(m+n)+m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times(m+n)+m</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times(m+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times(m+n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times q+(n-q)(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times q+(n-q)(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S2.T1.7.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:4pt;padding-bottom:4pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{k(k+1)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{k(k+1)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the state-of-the-art (SoTA) LoRA variants evaluated in this work. Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ II Related Work for Benchmarking ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> summarizes their corresponding formulations and trainable parameter counts. <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Trainable variables</span> in this study are indicated by <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlining</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LoRA</span>. LoRA freezes the pre-trained weights of models and injects trainable low-rank matrices into each linear layer. For a pre-trained weight <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, LoRA parameterizes its fine-tuning update as:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{W}_{0}+\underline{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐀𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{W}_{0}+\underline{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> are trainable low-rank matrices.
LoRA significantly reduces the number of trainable parameters to <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times(m+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times(m+n)</annotation></semantics></math> by choosing a rank <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(m,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(m,n)</annotation></semantics></math>, but as model size scales, it can still accumulate a non-trivial number of learnable parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VERA</span>. Unlike LoRA, which introduces trainable low-rank matrices for each adapted layer, VeRA employs a single pair of frozen, randomly initialized low-rank matrices shared across all layers. Adaptation is achieved through the learning of small, trainable scaling vectors specific to each layer. For a pre-trained weight <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, VERA adapt it as:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{W}_{0}+({\underline{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{d}}\mathbf{A})({\underline{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{b}}\mathbf{B})^{\top}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝚲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐀</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝚲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐁</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{W}_{0}+({\underline{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{d}}\mathbf{A})({\underline{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{b}}\mathbf{B})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> are low-rank random matrices, generated once and kept frozen. <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚲</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚲</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> are diagonal matrices formed from small trainable scaling vectors <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Since <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> are shared and reproducible from a fixed random seed, VeRA significantly lowers the memory footprint to <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DoRA</span>. While VeRA focuses on achieving decent performance with minimal trainable parameters, DoRA aims to bridge the performance gap between LoRA and full fine-tuning. For a pre-trained weight matrix <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, DoRA decomposes it into a magnitude vector <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}=\|\mathbf{W}_{0}\|_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}=\|\mathbf{W}_{0}\|_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|.\|_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.0835em">.</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|.\|_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vector-wise (column-wise) norm, and a directional matrix <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\mathbf{W}_{0}}{\|\mathbf{W}_{0}\|_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\mathbf{W}_{0}}{\|\mathbf{W}_{0}\|_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By analyzing the change in the components during LoRA tuning versus full fine-tuning, Liu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> observed that LoRA struggles to simultaneously learn both direction and scale, while full fine-tuning tends to adapt primarily one of the two. DoRA hereby simplifies optimization and focuses solely on directional adaptation, injecting low-rank updates only into the directional component:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{\underline{m}}\frac{\mathbf{W_{0}}+\mathbf{\underline{AB}}^{\top}}{\|\mathbf{W_{0}}+\mathbf{\underline{AB}}^{\top}\|_{c}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐀𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>𝟎</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐀𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{\underline{m}}\frac{\mathbf{W_{0}}+\mathbf{\underline{AB}}^{\top}}{\|\mathbf{W_{0}}+\mathbf{\underline{AB}}^{\top}\|_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Compared to LoRA, DoRA introduces an additional trainable magnitude vector <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing the trainable parameter count to <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\times(m+n)+m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\times(m+n)+m</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this increase remains negligible compared to the size of the full model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PiSSA</span>. Unlike LoRA, VeRA, and DoRA, which rely on randomly initialized low-rank matrices and suffer from inefficient early training or suboptimal local minima, PiSSA initializes the low-rank components <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> using the top-<math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> principal singular vectors and values from the SVD of the pre-trained weight matrix <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{0}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Given the SVD, with <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq n</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a feedforward layer):</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{0}=\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{\top}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sigma_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{0}=\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{\top}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sigma_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where, <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{U}\in\mathbb{R}^{m\times n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{V}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> contain the left and right singular vectors <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal matrix of singular values <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Fine-tuning is performed along the <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> principal directions, while the residual components with the <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math> smallest singular values are frozen:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\underline{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}}^{\top}+\sum_{i=k+1}^{n-k}\sigma_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐀𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\underline{\mathbf{A}\mathbf{B}}^{\top}+\sum_{i=k+1}^{n-k}\sigma_{i}\mathbf{u}_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> are initialized by <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}\sigma_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}\sigma_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> resp. <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{k}\sigma_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\mathrm{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{k}\sigma_{i}^{\frac{1}{2}}\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\mathrm{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This initialization enables more efficient optimization by aligning parameter updates with meaningful directions in the model’s parameter space, leading to faster convergence and improved performance compared to standard LoRA.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SVFT</span>. SVFT extends PiSSA by further reducing the number of trainable parameters, modifying only a sparse subset of singular values and associated directions. Starting from the SVD of a pre-trained weight matrix (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In II Related Work for Benchmarking ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>),
SVFT updates the weight as:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{U}(\mathbf{\Sigma}+\underline{\mathbf{M}})\mathbf{V}^{\top}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝚺</mi><mo>+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐌</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}^{\prime}=\mathbf{U}(\mathbf{\Sigma}+\underline{\mathbf{M}})\mathbf{V}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a small, learnable matrix. Different structures of <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> lead to different SVFT variants:
1) <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SVFT^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SVFT^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> uses a diagonal matrix <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, adapting only singular values (akin to reweighting frozen singular directions);
2) <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SVFT_{d}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SVFT_{d}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> uses a banded matrix to introduce learnable off-diagonal interactions;
3) <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SVFT_{d}^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>R</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SVFT_{d}^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math> as a fixed random matrix;
4) <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SVFT_{d}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SVFT_{d}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> makes the top-<math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> strong interactions between singular vector directions learnable.
These variants enable SVFT to explicitly adjust the most critical directions in the parameter space. Among them, <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SVFT_{d}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SVFT_{d}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> demonstrated the best performance in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, and has a trainable parameter count of <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times q+(n-q)(q+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times q+(n-q)(q+1)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the bandwidth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Although these methods have been extensively studied in text and vision domains, their effectiveness in speech remains largely unexplored. Moreover, none of them is fundamentally tailored to the unique characteristics of speech signals. This limitation is particularly evident in SVD-guided methods, which explicitly decompose the input and output feature spaces—an operation linked to the acoustic and semantic properties of speech.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In the following section, we highlight this intrinsic nature of SVD-based methods and introduce structured SVD-Guided fine-tuning (SSVD), which explicitly adapts the input feature space to enable efficient and robust domain adaptation in speech.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SSVD Method</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As SVD is mathematically demonstrated in Section <a href="#S2" title="II Related Work for Benchmarking ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> - <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PiSSA</span> (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In II Related Work for Benchmarking ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), each right singular vector <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to the corresponding left singular vector <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, scaled by the singular value <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚺</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An input <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, represented in the coordinate system spanned by the right singular vector <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, is mapped to an output <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\operatorname{Col}\mathbf{O}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Col</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝐎</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\operatorname{Col}\mathbf{O}\subset\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> in the coordinate system spanned by the left singular vector <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{\top}\mathbf{x}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{\Sigma}\mathbf{V}^{\top}\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under domain shift in the speech input space, the input <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is no longer aligned with the original right singular basis <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, but instead aligned with a shifted basis <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{i}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. This constitutes an “inner” transform, whereas the output semantic space, governed by <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, remains unchanged (the “outer” transform). The shift in coordinate basis can be modeled through a rotation and scaling of the original input space. Accordingly, the transformation becomes:</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{(}{\Sigma+\underline{\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}}})\underline{\mathbf{G}}\mathbf{V}^{\top}\mathbf{x}\\
=\mathbf{W}^{\prime}\mathbf{x}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐔</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>𝚫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>𝐆</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐕</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐖</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{U}\boldsymbol{(}{\Sigma+\underline{\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}}})\underline{\mathbf{G}}\mathbf{V}^{\top}\mathbf{x}\\
=\mathbf{W}^{\prime}\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where, the diagonal matrix <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> models axis-wise scaling (i.e., singular value shifts), and <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is an orthogonal matrix representing a series of rotations in the right singular vector space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since the principal singular values and vectors for the best matrix approximation (Eckart-Young theorem) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, we use</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}_{k}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}~~\text{and}~~\mathbf{G}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{G}_{k}&amp;0\\
0&amp;I\end{bmatrix}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝚺</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝚫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚺</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐆</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{\Delta\Sigma}_{k}&amp;0\\
0&amp;0\end{bmatrix}~~\text{and}~~\mathbf{G}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{G}_{k}&amp;0\\
0&amp;I\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">to apply adaptations to the top-<math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> components. In Section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="V-A ASR performance ‣ V Results ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-A</span></a>, we discuss different choices for the proportion of adapted components.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To implement rotation within the inner transformation, we explore three parameterizations of the transformation matrix <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which trade off orthogonality constraints for computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Strict orthogonal constraint</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We enforce strict orthogonality by defining <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> using the Cayley transform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{k}=(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{K})(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{K})^{-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{k}=(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{K})(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{K})^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐊</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> is a skew-symmetric matrix (i.e, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}^{T}=-\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}^{T}=-\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math>). By learning a skew-symmetric matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number of trainable parameters in SSVD is reduced to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\frac{k(k-1)}{2}+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\frac{k(k-1)}{2}+k)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is significantly fewer than the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k^{2}+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k^{2}+k)</annotation></semantics></math> parameters required to directly learn a full matrix <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (‘<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+k</annotation></semantics></math>’ accounts for singular values scaling update).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the matrix inversion in the Cayley transform incurs a computational cost of approximately <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(k^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(k^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> cost, which becomes prohibitive for large dimensions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. To address this, we introduce an approximate orthogonality constraint using a first–order Cayley approximation in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="III-B Approximate orthogonal constraint ‣ III SSVD Method ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Approximate orthogonal constraint</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\mathbf{K}\rVert\!\ll\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐊</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.0539em">∥</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.0539em">≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\mathbf{K}\rVert\!\ll\!1</annotation></semantics></math>, we can approximate the inverse in the Cayley transform via a truncated Neumann series:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{K})^{-1}=\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{K}+\mathcal{O}(\mathbf{K}^{2})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{I}+\mathbf{K})^{-1}=\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{K}+\mathcal{O}(\mathbf{K}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">leading to a first-order approximation:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{k}\;\approx\;(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{K})(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{K})=\mathbf{I}-2\mathbf{K}+\mathcal{O}(\mathbf{K}^{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{k}\;\approx\;(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{K})(\mathbf{I}-\mathbf{K})=\mathbf{I}-2\mathbf{K}+\mathcal{O}(\mathbf{K}^{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By keeping only the linear term, we obtain the simplified form:</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{k}\approx\mathbf{I}-2\mathbf{K}\," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐊</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{k}\approx\mathbf{I}-2\mathbf{K}\,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which avoids matrix inversion and reduces the cost to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(k^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(k^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. This approximation introduces an orthogonality error of order <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lVert\mathbf{K}\rVert^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝐊</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lVert\mathbf{K}\rVert^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is often acceptable for small <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, a common setting in PEFT. We use <span id="S3.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">approximate orthogonality constraint</span> by default in this study. In Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="V-C Ablation study ‣ V Results ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-C</span></a>, we compare the strict and approximate orthogonality settings and demonstrate that the resulting error is trivial.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Unconstrained rotations</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a further relaxation, we drop the orthogonality constraint altogether, allowing <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{G}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐆</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{G}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{k\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>
to be a freely parameterized matrix. This maximally reduces computational overhead but sacrifices the beneficial geometric properties of orthogonal transformations, such as preserving norms and angles. It also increases the number of trainable parameters to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k^{2}+k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k^{2}+k)</annotation></semantics></math>. The performance of this unconstrained rotation is also evaluated in Section <a href="#S5.SS3" title="V-C Ablation study ‣ V Results ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we choose the open Whisper-style speech models OWSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> as our initial models, as they support a wide range of model scales, from 0.1B to 18B parameters. Moreover, the OWSM series is fully open-sourced, providing researchers with detailed information about the corpora used in pre-trainig. This transparency ensures that there is no domain overlap in our evaluation setup, allowing for a reliable study of domain-shift issues in ASR.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All PEFT methods are implemented within the ESPnet framework, we evaluate them by fine-tuning OWSM models at 0.1B, 1B, and 2B (OWLS) scales on two domain-shifted speech datasets: MyST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> (child speech) and CGN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> (dialectal Flemish and Dutch).</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dataset</span></h3>

