<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OWSM uses an E-Branchformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> encoder with a Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> decoder, while OWLS is a standard Transformer encoder–decoder. Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-B Implementation details ‣ IV Experiments ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> lists their full configurations. OWSM and OWLS models are trained on 180k hours of public speech data, as documented in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, which do not include child speech or Flemish Dutch, although standard Dutch data are included. Therefore, domain-shifted scenarios are expected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our study, PEFT methods are applied to all linear layers in each model, including query, key, and value projections. For different low-rank configurations, we denote them as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LoRA_{r=rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LoRA_{r=rank}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VeRA_{r=rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VeRA_{r=rank}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="DoRA_{r=rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">DoRA_{r=rank}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PiSSA_{r=rank}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PiSSA_{r=rank}</annotation></semantics></math>. For SVFT, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> and use the best-performing banded matrix <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SVFT_{d=band~size}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.270em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SVFT_{d=band~size}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math>. For SSVD, with different choices of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In III SSVD Method ‣ SSVD: Structured SVD for Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning and Benchmarking under Domain Shift in ASR" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), rotation adaptations are applied to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the right singular vectors and values, denoted as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SSVD_{p=portion}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SSVD_{p=portion}</annotation></semantics></math>. All experiments are conducted on a single NVIDIA H100 80GB GPU.</p>
</div>
